    "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the measure of an angle whose measure is double the measure of its complement. Let's denote the measure of the angle by \\( x \\) degrees.\n\n1. **Understanding Complementary Angles:**\n   - Two angles are complementary if their measures add up to 90 degrees.\n   - Therefore, if the measure of the angle is \\( x \\), then the measure of its complement is \\( 90 - x \\).\n\n2. **Setting Up the Equation:**\n   - According to the problem, the measure of the angle is double the measure of its complement.\n   - This relationship can be expressed as:\n     \\[\n     x = 2 \\times (90 - x)\n     \\]\n\n3. **Solving the Equation:**\n   - First, distribute the 2 on the right-hand side:\n     \\[\n     x = 180 - 2x\n     \\]\n   - Next, add \\( 2x \\) to both sides to isolate \\( x \\) on one side:\n     \\[\n     x + 2x = 180\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3x = 180\n     \\]\n   - Finally, divide both sides by 3 to solve for \\( x \\):\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{180}{3}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = 60\n     \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - The measure of the angle is \\( 60 \\) degrees.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{60}\n\\]",
